# Answer 14 · Gender Studies versus Women's Studies; the Multidisciplinary Nature of Gender Studies (CSS 2023)
@py question 2023-3

## The answer map
::: fig mindmap | The answer map: every branch is one numbered heading of the model answer below.
@note Two parts: differentiate briefly with a table, then discuss the many disciplines "in detail" — the heavier part.
@ask ① DIFFERENTIATE between Gender Studies and Women's Studies
@ask ② DISCUSS IN DETAIL the multidisciplinary nature of Gender Studies
(1) Introduction: WS puts women at the centre; GS studies relations; GS uses many disciplines
(2) Women's Studies: born 1969–70; women as subject, knower, beneficiary
(3) Gender Studies: grew from the 1980s; includes men and sexualities
(4) The differences: table of subject, idea, origin, viewpoint, theories
(5) What they share: feminist roots; criticism of male-centred knowledge
(6) Why many disciplines: gender is everywhere; problems are complex
(7) The disciplines: from sociology to religious studies, each with a thinker
(8) A Pakistani example: low female employment needs six disciplines
(9) Mixing disciplines: new ideas like the care economy
(10) Strengths and weaknesses: full picture vs no clear home
(11) Conclusion: many disciplines are the field's method
:::

## Decoding the question
Two command words. **"Differentiate"** asks for clear differences — a table works best. **"Discuss in detail"** asks for a full account of the multidisciplinary nature: what it means, why the field has it, what each discipline adds, and what it costs. Give about **one-third** of the answer to the first part and **two-thirds** to the second, which "in detail" marks as heavier.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definitions
**Women's Studies** is the field, begun around ==1969–70== as "**the academic arm of the women's movement**", that puts **women at the centre** of study. **Gender Studies** grew out of it from the 1980s and studies how society makes femininity and masculinity and how this shares out power among **women, men and gender-diverse people**.

#### ii) The argument
The two differ in subject and key idea but share a feminist commitment. What most marks out Gender Studies is its **multidisciplinary** nature: it studies gender with the tools of every field that gender touches — which is **every field**.

### 2. Women's Studies

#### i) Origin
It began with a course at **Cornell** (==1969==), the first programme at **San Diego State** (==1970==) and the **National Women's Studies Association** (==1977==).

#### ii) Features
It made women the **subject** of study, the **knowers** whose experience counts, and the **beneficiaries** of research. Its question was: *where are the women?*

### 3. Gender Studies

#### i) Origin
From the 1980s the idea of **gender** (**Ann Oakley**, ==1972==; **Joan Scott**, ==1986==), **men's studies** (**Raewyn Connell**, ==1987==) and **queer theory** (about ==1990==) widened the field.

#### ii) Features
It studies **gender relations**, including **men, masculinities and sexualities**, and gender as a **structure of power**.

#### iii) In Pakistan
Pakistan's centres of ==1989== began as "**Women's Studies**" centres; most are now "**Gender Studies**" departments.

### 4. The differences

::: table | Women's Studies and Gender Studies compared.
@widths 2.6,5.7,5.7
Point | Women's Studies | Gender Studies
Subject | Women's lives, history and experience | Relations between women, men and gender-diverse people
Key idea | "Woman"; women's oppression | Gender as made by society and as power
Origin | Women's movement, late 1960s | Gender theory, men's studies, queer theory, 1980s–90s
Viewpoint | By and for women | Many viewpoints, including men's
Theories | Liberal, radical, socialist feminism | Adds constructionism, queer theory, intersectionality
Honour killing | Studies the victims and failures of justice | Also studies *ghairat*, manhood and family bargaining
:::

### 5. What they share

Both share **feminist roots**, a rejection of **male-centred knowledge**, the use of **many disciplines** and an **aim of change**. **Tania Modleski**'s warning in *Feminism Without Women* (==1991==) — that "gender" could make women invisible again — explains why many departments use the joint name "**Women's and Gender Studies**".

### 6. Why Gender Studies uses many disciplines

#### i) Definitions
**Multidisciplinary** means several disciplines studying gender **side by side**. As the field has grown it has also become **interdisciplinary** — **mixing** them into new ideas.

#### ii) Reasons
1. **Gender is everywhere** — bodies, minds, families, economies, laws, religions and languages.
2. **Its problems cross boundaries**; no single discipline can solve them.
3. **Its founders came from many departments**.
4. **To expose male bias in a subject, you must work inside it.**

### 7. What each discipline adds

::: fig grid | The disciplines of Gender Studies, each with its key thinker.
@cols 4
Sociology: housework is real work (Oakley)
Anthropology: gender differs by culture (Mead)
Psychology: androgyny; ethic of care (Bem; Gilligan)
History: patriarchy had a beginning (Lerner)
Economics: unpaid work counts (Boserup; Waring)
Political science: the hidden sexual contract (Pateman)
Law: harassment is discrimination (MacKinnon)
Biology and medicine: sex, intersex, women's health
Literature and language: women writers; biased words (Showalter; Lakoff)
Media: the male gaze (Mulvey)
Development: gender planning (Moser; Kabeer)
Religious studies: re-reading holy texts (Wadud; Mernissi)
:::

#### i) Sociology and anthropology
**Sociology** shows that roles are made by society; **Ann Oakley** (==1974==) showed housework is real work. **Anthropology** proves that gender varies: **Margaret Mead**'s **Arapesh, Mundugumor and Tchambuli** (==1935==); **Sherry Ortner**'s link between women and "nature" (==1974==).

#### ii) Psychology and history
**Psychology** studies identity and stereotypes — **Sandra Bem**'s **androgyny** (==1974==), **Carol Gilligan**'s **ethic of care** (==1982==). **History** recovers women's past and shows patriarchy had a **beginning** (**Gerda Lerner**, ==1986==).

#### iii) Economics, politics and law
**Economics** measures women's work — **Ester Boserup** (==1970==) on women farmers, **Marilyn Waring** (==1988==) on unpaid labour. **Political science and law** study power and rights — **Carole Pateman**'s *The Sexual Contract* (==1988==), **Catharine MacKinnon** on harassment (==1979==), and **CEDAW** (==1979==).

#### iv) Biology, medicine and culture
**Biology and medicine** give the facts of sex, variation and **women's health** — in Pakistan, ==186== maternal deaths per 100,000 live births (PMMS 2019). **Literature, language and media** study representation — Showalter's **gynocriticism**, Lakoff's "**women's language**" (==1975==), Mulvey's "**male gaze**" (==1975==).

#### v) Development and religion
**Development studies** turn analysis into practice — **Moser**'s gender planning (==1993==), **Kabeer**'s empowerment. **Religious studies** re-read Islamic texts from a woman's view — **Amina Wadud** and **Fatima Mernissi**.

### 8. A Pakistani example

Only ==24.4%== of working-age Pakistani women are in the labour force (**Labour Force Survey 2024–25**). **Economics** measures the gap; **sociology** explains family rules on women's work; **law** examines harassment and maternity protection; **planning** studies unsafe transport; **psychology** studies women's fear of public space; **political science** asks whether policy-makers care. No single discipline could explain the problem — which is exactly why Gender Studies must use many. And no single ministry could solve it: the answer needs education, labour, transport, law and social welfare working together — the policy mirror of a multidisciplinary field.

### 9. Mixing disciplines: new ideas

The disciplines do not only sit side by side; they **blend**. When economists and sociologists work together on unpaid care, the idea of the **care economy** is born. When lawyers and doctors work together on the medical evidence in rape trials, **survivor-centred justice** develops. This move from multidisciplinary to **interdisciplinary** work has produced the field's most original ideas, such as **intersectionality** and **gender budgeting**.

### 10. Strengths and weaknesses

#### i) Strengths
A **complete picture**; **new ideas**; direct **usefulness for policy**; many research methods.

#### ii) Weaknesses
Critics say the field has **no core method**; teachers are often trained in **one discipline**; number-based and story-based methods can **clash**.

#### iii) The link to the autonomy debate
Many disciplines raise the question of the field's **home** — the **autonomy versus integration debate**. Because gender belongs to every subject, some want it **integrated**; because it has its own ideas, others want **separate departments**. The best answer for Pakistan combines the two.

### 11. Conclusion
Women's Studies and Gender Studies differ in **subject and key idea** — women, or gender relations — but form one intellectual project. Gender Studies' defining feature is its **multidisciplinary nature**: sociology, anthropology, psychology, history, economics, politics, law, biology, literature, media, development and religious studies each supply a piece of the picture. For the examiner, the key point is that using many disciplines is **not a weakness to excuse but the field's method** — gender can only be understood where the disciplines meet. In Pakistan, where gender shapes every social problem from maternal deaths to low female employment, that combined picture is the foundation of good policy.

## Why this answer scores
- The weighting follows the command words: a **short table** for "differentiate", a **detailed** treatment for "discuss in detail".
- Every discipline is linked to a **named thinker with a date**; a **grid figure** shows the whole part at a glance.
- The **Pakistani example** shows why many disciplines matter in practice.
- The section on **mixing disciplines** shows understanding beyond a list.
- The link to the **autonomy debate** shows command of the whole topic.
